        "delegation_parent": "R/A",
        "integration_sources": [],
        "hypothesis_as_declared_by_model": "A compact position-only array should improve locality in the all-pairs force traversal by reducing the source data footprint from six floats to three floats per body, while retaining the original ascending j order and arithmetic for each body's force accumulation.",
        "transformations_as_declared_by_model": {
          "files": [
            "src/nbody.c"
          ],
          "retained": [
            "Introduce a compact Position array initialized from the input Body array.",
            "Read positions from the compact array during force calculation and update it during position integration; preserve the original force accumulation order and synchronize positions back before output."
          ]
        },
        "status": "valid",
        "mean_seconds": 0.665449951775372,
        "commit": "10f85d3a5f45307aa7a9b05b709f5cd1062a0ff7"
      },
      {
        "id": "ca72f94cea6241f1",
        "role": "B",
        "parent_id": null,
        "delegation_parent": "R/B",
        "integration_sources": [],
        "hypothesis_as_declared_by_model": "SIMD-vectorizing the outer body loop may evaluate forces for several independent target bodies concurrently while leaving each target's j-loop accumulation order unchanged, reducing force-computation time without a floating-point reduction reassociation.",
        "transformations_as_declared_by_model": {
          "files": [
            "src/nbody.c"
          ],
          "retained": [
            "Added an OpenMP SIMD directive to the outer target-body loop in body_force; retained the serial inner source-body loop and its accumulation order."
          ]
        },
        "status": "valid",
        "mean_seconds": 0.665468191417555,
        "commit": "8031541e66b45c6f384a72c78e51c978952e1c43"
      },
      {
        "id": "a841149e87744055",
        "role": "C",
        "parent_id": null,
        "delegation_parent": "R/C",
        "integration_sources": [],
        "hypothesis_as_declared_by_model": "A persistent OpenMP team with static worksharing across independent bodies should parallelize the dominant pair-force calculation while avoiding repeated team creation across simulation iterations. Static scheduling fits the uniform per-body workload; preserving each body's increasing-j accumulation order should retain reference output semantics.",
        "transformations_as_declared_by_model": {
          "files": [
            "src/nbody.c"
          ],
          "retained": [
            "Create one OpenMP parallel region around all simulation iterations instead of creating teams for each work phase.",
            "Use schedule(static) for outer-body force calculations and position updates, leaving the inner interaction loop serial and in its original order."
          ]
        },
        "status": "valid",
        "mean_seconds": 0.17229464246580997,
        "commit": "f2bc84928dbee49247416733705942babc7b0729"
      },
      {
        "id": "9e30053260324084",
        "role": "A",
        "parent_id": null,
        "delegation_parent": "R/A",
        "integration_sources": [],
        "hypothesis_as_declared_by_model": "Grouping four target bodies in the force traversal should improve locality by reusing each source body's position loads across several targets, while retaining ascending source order and the original per-target force accumulation sequence.",
        "transformations_as_declared_by_model": {
          "files": [
            "src/nbody.c"
          ],
          "retained": [
            "Tile the target-body loop in groups of four and load each target's position once per tile.",
            "Interchange the target and source traversal within each tile so source positions are loaded once and reused across the tile's targets.",
            "Keep each target's source loop in ascending j order and apply its velocity update only after its force accumulation."
          ]
        },
        "status": "valid",
        "mean_seconds": 0.9054509385799369,
        "commit": "ba4261a99ebe2465f2c4c05e746604574718b062"
      },
      {
        "id": "8dabc6166365471e",
        "role": "B",
        "parent_id": "ca72f94cea6241f1",
        "delegation_parent": "R/B",
        "integration_sources": [],
        "hypothesis_as_declared_by_model": "Processing four target bodies as SIMD lanes can reuse each source body's coordinates and vectorize force calculations while preserving the original serial j-order accumulation independently for every target, avoiding a floating-point reduction reassociation.",
        "transformations_as_declared_by_model": {
          "files": [
            "src/nbody.c"
          ],
          "retained": [
            "Replace the outer-target SIMD annotation with four-target tiling and an OpenMP SIMD lane loop inside the serial source-body loop.",
            "Keep each target's j traversal and force accumulation order unchanged, and retain a scalar tail for body counts not divisible by four."
          ]
        },
        "status": "valid",
        "mean_seconds": 0.8448957571138939,
        "commit": "42be9cc7c87f5a68a8bdaa286790ed405fbcc671"
      },
      {
        "id": "ae2aedf049404be5",
        "role": "C",
        "parent_id": null,
        "delegation_parent": "R/C",
        "integration_sources": [],
        "hypothesis_as_declared_by_model": "A body-count cutover avoids OpenMP team and synchronization overhead for small simulations while retaining a persistent team with static worksharing for larger inputs. Each body's force accumulation remains in increasing-j order, preserving the reference arithmetic order.",
        "transformations_as_declared_by_model": {
          "files": [
            "src/nbody.c"
          ],
          "retained": [
            "Extract the per-body force calculation so serial and parallel paths use the same increasing-j accumulation sequence.",
            "Use a serial simulation below 128 bodies; at or above the cutover, use one persistent OpenMP team with static worksharing for force and position loops across all iterations."
          ]
        },
        "status": "valid",
        "mean_seconds": 0.1749869423607985,
        "commit": "574243d407df7ad4b0f8bfd48d95f21cae1b51ad"
      },
